#!/bin/bash
# A code review of a change, from Astra 6.2 at xhigh in the Codex CLI
# (Ben, 2026-11-27: builders get code reviews from Codex Astra 6.2 on xhigh
# (max 2 rounds) alongside Antigravity Gemini 4 Neon on xhigh (max 2 rounds);
# changes with visual design work also get Claude Opus 5.5 on xhigh). Every
# change gets reviewed before it is released: what is wrong in the code (logic,
# error paths, races, waits with no end, what an older build meeting a newer
# one breaks), what it breaks elsewhere, the project's rules, and what its
# tests don't hold, each with a case in which it goes wrong.
#
#   tools/dev/astra-review [--about "WHAT IT IS, WHAT CHANGED"] [--tree DIR] RANGE [PATH…]
#   tools/dev/astra-review [--about …] [--tree DIR] --working [PATH…]
#
# RANGE: the commits to review, as git names them (`main~2..main`,
# `v2026.11.27.7..HEAD`). --working: what isn't committed yet, new files
# too (PATHs: only those; name them where others are at work in the
# tree). --tree: another checkout than this one. It reads the diff and
# whatever it needs of the tree, runs nothing and changes nothing; its
# review is printed and kept, with the diff it read, in a folder of its
# own under /var/tmp/olo-reviews. The same change asked again is its
# review as written, not a second one. Check each thing it says against
# the code yourself, fix what's real, and say in the commit what it
# found and what you did.
#
# The review is the Codex CLI in a child tab of the minder/lead (a tmux
# session where you aren't in a Rex pane), as every agent here is
# (`tools/dev/agents review --by astra`: this is that), so it can be read
# while it works (`tools/dev/agents peek astra-…`) and told more (`agents say`).
# Run this in the background: it prints a line a minute of what the
# review is doing, and returns when the review is written (0), or as
# soon as it needs you (3: answer it, then run this again with the same
# arguments to go on waiting).
exec /usr/bin/python3 "$(dirname "$0")/agents" review --by astra "$@"
